60
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:50,800
Yeah.

61
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,160
The grid is changing.

62
00:02:52,526 --> 00:02:53,280
Can we talk about that?

63
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,040
Can you tell me your perspective on it?

64
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:01,188
Our energy grid has been in a very predictable world in the past decades.

65
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:05,310
every year the load grows at 1 to 2%, you know, single digit.

66
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:11,920
so at a gradual change, utilities was able to plan ahead with good confidence.

67
00:03:12,206 --> 00:03:14,480
But this playbook no longer works now.

68
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:20,320
You know, we see AI data centers, electrification loads, and advanced manufacture.

69
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:24,000
They all come at the same time and they're arriving fast.

70
00:03:24,206 --> 00:03:27,300
So this is not just another gross load cycle.

71
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:29,360
This is a structural shift.

72
00:03:29,614 --> 00:03:39,680
You know, the real problem we're dealing with today is not only how do we provide the power to support them, it is also when, where, and how we deliver those power.

73
00:03:40,718 --> 00:03:44,612
And AI data centers, how big a deal are they when it comes to grid operations?

74
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:50,944
Oh, a lot of people think AI data centers are just another big customers.

75
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:52,196
Are they not?

76
00:03:52,544 --> 00:03:54,340
No, they're really not.

77
00:03:54,944 --> 00:04:03,904
think about this difference between having one household in your neighborhood versus having the entire city block overnight.

78
00:04:04,238 --> 00:04:06,624
So data center loads are like that.

79
00:04:06,784 --> 00:04:15,652
They consume enormous amount of energy in a very concentrated location, and then they are all gets deployed at an unforeseeable speed.

80
00:04:15,904 --> 00:04:21,184
So this will definitely change the way we operate our grid today.

81
00:04:21,613 --> 00:04:26,144
And you'd say that at the moment the grid has got a lack of power, or was it something else?

82
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:29,744
Is that why we're having to invest so much into our networks?

83
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:37,344
Yeah, people tend to think that the problem here is that we don't have enough electricity to support those data centers.

84
00:04:37,646 --> 00:04:47,984
But in many cases, this actually is not the biggest issue because the data centers, you know, come in a very different way, uh, unforeseable in the past.

85
00:04:48,304 --> 00:05:00,292
So the way our grid accommodated is that you know, whenever we connect new generations, connect a new large data center load, we'll have to go through extensive engineering studies.

86
00:05:00,624 --> 00:05:06,244
So we can't connect new generations quick enough to support the AI data center loads.

87
00:05:06,704 --> 00:05:10,340
So the real bottleneck here is the analysis.

88
00:05:10,734 --> 00:05:13,664
Analysis to support the decision-making process here.

89
00:05:14,064 --> 00:05:14,224
Okay.

90
00:05:14,510 --> 00:05:16,164
Where does software come into play?

91
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:21,252
So every important grid decision starts with a simulation.

92
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:25,584
For example, can we support a new data center load?

93
00:05:25,824 --> 00:05:27,904
Can we connect a new generation?

94
00:05:28,144 --> 00:05:30,944
Will my grid still remain stable, right?

95
00:05:31,264 --> 00:05:39,184
So if simulation requires days, weeks, or years to run, then all the energy project has to wait for that.

96
00:05:39,470 --> 00:05:51,024
So it's like we have generations, new generations to support the load, but it's like you have airplanes lying in the wrong way, but the air traffic control have to clear them one by one.

97
00:05:51,406 --> 00:05:54,304
So software becomes a real bottleneck here.

98
00:05:54,464 --> 00:05:56,224
It's the speed of analysis.

99
00:05:56,590 --> 00:06:05,412
So because those decisions are moving at the speed of simulation, I guess AI can come in and help speed this up and help with the admin of it.

100
00:06:06,144 --> 00:06:06,384
Yeah.

101
00:06:06,464 --> 00:06:09,924
I think AI can play a important role here.

102
00:06:10,574 --> 00:06:23,664
But the role of AI is not to replace engineers, but to support them, accelerate the decision making so that they can analyze much more possibilities and much faster.

103
00:06:23,918 --> 00:06:29,984
So things that used to take, let's say, weeks or months to run now can be completed within minutes.

104
00:06:30,190 --> 00:06:33,024
And this is not just saving engineering time.

105
00:06:33,198 --> 00:06:37,024
This is also accelerate the whole infrastructure project timeline.

106
00:06:37,424 --> 00:06:45,184
So I get that AI helps you do the things that you spend a lot of time doing that, you know, you can delegate to an automated system.

107
00:06:46,144 --> 00:06:52,864
Some might argue that there is also some people who might try to humanize AI a bit more than they should.

108
00:06:52,944 --> 00:06:55,664
And you've just said, you know, AI is not here to replace humans.

109
00:06:55,886 --> 00:06:56,704
I'm on board with that.

110
00:06:57,344 --> 00:07:05,104
So, but what does the expression human-centered AI-enabled grid, which is an expression that is coming up more and more often?

111
00:07:05,344 --> 00:07:05,904
What does it mean?

112
00:07:06,064 --> 00:07:07,024
What do we mean by that?

113
00:07:07,424 --> 00:07:09,104
Yeah, that's a great question.

114
00:07:09,264 --> 00:07:18,704
I think human-centered grid to me actually means that we're designing, integrating AI to work with the engineer, not to around them.

115
00:07:18,864 --> 00:07:35,492
So humans do remains in control, but AI can help them surface options, visualize the trade offs, and minimize the complexity so that the engineers can make better decisions much faster and with greater confidence.

116
00:07:37,008 --> 00:07:40,688
So our industry, the energy industry, is very conservative for good reason.

117
00:07:40,848 --> 00:07:42,608
You know, we need to keep the grid reliable.

118
00:07:42,688 --> 00:07:44,368
It needs to keep running, giving us energy.

119
00:07:44,928 --> 00:07:50,288
So the customers that you're working with, are they risk averse when it comes to AI?

120
00:07:50,688 --> 00:07:57,488
Or do you find that this slightly older generation now who has been working in the industry for many, many years, is actually welcoming of it?

121
00:07:57,888 --> 00:08:02,608
The energy industry is known for being conservative for good reasons.

122
00:08:02,768 --> 00:08:02,928
Yeah.

123
00:08:03,248 --> 00:08:06,768
Because reliability is a mandate here, right?

124
00:08:07,088 --> 00:08:11,332
Because, you know, losing reliability will impact millions of people's lives.

125
00:08:11,918 --> 00:08:20,048
However, in the past decades, we start to see the industry become more open-minded, especially to new technologies.

126
00:08:20,334 --> 00:08:21,668
AI is one of them.

127
00:08:22,048 --> 00:08:31,968
And the users, you know, daily users in the energy industry also include a lot of younger generation who are more tech-svy, right?

128
00:08:32,048 --> 00:08:38,852
So they demand more convenience, more uh insights-driven solutions, software designing.

129
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:56,036
So that also evolves how manufacturer and software vendor like Hitachi develop our digital solutions because you know, human-centered design is always a philosophy that we're proud of ourselves, right?

130
00:08:56,448 --> 00:09:08,996
So in addition to designing, innovating in the physical AI world, where we commit to make our AI solutions reliable, repeatable, and auditable.

131
00:09:09,488 --> 00:09:29,700
But we're also incorporating the latest human-centered software designing philosophy, so that they're easy to use, and they're insights-driven, so that the user can focus on generating the insights, making the decision, rather than wrestling with the buttons and the clicks.

132
00:09:30,766 --> 00:09:36,260
Can you give me some real life examples of projects where you're seeing AI doing exactly that?

133
00:09:36,622 --> 00:09:37,488
Helping engineers?

134
00:09:37,888 --> 00:09:48,928
So, for example, last year I was working on an important proposal, which was tens of millions of revenue.

135
00:09:49,168 --> 00:09:54,980
So, to work on something like that, you cannot just put your fantasy in it.

136
00:09:55,168 --> 00:10:03,972
So you need to ground your proposal solutions with very well-rounded scientific facts.

137
00:10:04,368 --> 00:10:19,008
And AI can help you do literature research, can help you put together the work plan, can help you check what's missing, can help you align with the competitors, can help you evaluate the markets.

138
00:10:19,278 --> 00:10:29,728
You know, this is like using AI as a personal assistant so that you get all the you know great solutions lined up in one proposal.

139
00:10:29,888 --> 00:10:35,248
You know, with the help of AI, you save a lot of time doing the researching by yourself.

140
00:10:35,408 --> 00:10:46,288
It will also help you improve the productivity and of course, you know, the write-up, the editing, you know, those are the uh given with the latest agentic AI tools.

141
00:10:46,448 --> 00:10:50,128
I could have done with something like that when I was writing my dissertation a few years back.

142
00:10:50,448 --> 00:10:51,088
Exactly.

143
00:10:51,328 --> 00:10:53,968
Just the footnotes and the references.

144
00:10:54,208 --> 00:10:54,608
Yeah.

145
00:10:54,928 --> 00:10:58,368
So we've got innovation, right, which is what we're about at Hitachi.

146
00:10:58,768 --> 00:11:00,688
And then we've also got reliability and cost.

147
00:11:00,928 --> 00:11:02,404
How do you bring the three together?

148
00:11:02,702 --> 00:11:03,088
Yeah.

149
00:11:03,438 --> 00:11:04,368
How do you balance them?

150
00:11:04,608 --> 00:11:04,928
Yes.

151
00:11:05,248 --> 00:11:07,728
Energy industry is a very unique industry.

152
00:11:07,968 --> 00:11:13,796
Reliability always comes the first because millions of people depend on it every day.

153
00:11:14,288 --> 00:11:19,428
but good news is that innovation and reliability are not conflicting goals.

154
00:11:19,662 --> 00:11:35,648
So with better decision support AI tools, with faster computing, and then with smarter decision-enabling solutions, we can support the human to make better decisions with great confidence.

155
00:11:36,110 --> 00:11:41,328
If I can ask you about a project specifically, would you tell our listeners about the Southwest Power Pool project?

156
00:11:41,646 --> 00:11:44,288
You know, it's a real example that I'd like you to take us through.

157
00:11:44,608 --> 00:11:45,248
Yeah.

158
00:11:45,488 --> 00:11:54,020
Um so SPP, you know, Southwest Power Pool, they're one of the large powerpool operators in the US.

159
00:11:54,528 --> 00:11:59,172
but one of their important role is to maintain the regional reliability.

160
00:12:00,128 --> 00:12:07,748
so they are facing an industry wide challenge these days called a generation interconnection backlog.

161
00:12:08,334 --> 00:12:14,436
You know, in many cases, people think we don't have enough electricity to support the load growth.

162
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:20,800
But the truth is that we need to connect the new generation onto the grid.

163
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:31,040
And connecting the new generation and AI data center load takes too long because each project will require extensive engineering studies.

164
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:46,240
So meanwhile, you know, the growth of the demand, especially driven by the AI data center load, they get deployed so fast that the analysis, the studies cannot be done quick enough so that we cannot bring the new generation online to support them.

165
00:12:46,414 --> 00:12:51,076
So the real bottleneck here is the analysis, not the electricity.

166
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:56,484
So working with the SPP, we're tasked to rethink this process.

167
00:12:57,280 --> 00:13:02,960
Can we develop new technologies to accelerate the decision process?

168
00:13:03,406 --> 00:13:15,440
So we developed a physics AI-driven approach, combining machine learning with engineering principles so that we can help the stakeholders make decisions faster.

169
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:29,920
So the goal here is not only to perform those engineering studies faster, but also perform them much faster, but without losing the engineering rigors that utility will require to have.

170
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:38,880
So working with SPP under this one hit Haji team, we're able to dramatically accelerate the studies.

171
00:13:39,342 --> 00:13:45,920
For example, contingency analysis used to require weeks to run, now can be completed within minutes.

172
00:13:46,190 --> 00:13:47,920
This is a hundred times speed up.

173
00:13:48,240 --> 00:14:00,800
And also, you know, together with some other AI tools, we're able to accelerate the generation review process, uh, shrink the total process from 18 months to within a year.

174
00:14:01,038 --> 00:14:06,916
So this will definitely help accelerate the yeah, bring more generations online much sooner.

175
00:14:07,566 --> 00:14:10,800
So with AI, you move from insight to action a lot quicker.

176
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:11,760
Exactly.

177
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:26,480
I think that is the real um real deal here, because the future of the industry is not just about, you know, building new hardware, modernize the infrastructure, but also help the industry members to move from insights to action much faster.

178
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:31,280
Your Southwest PowerPool project, the advancements that you've created are just phenomenal.

179
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:33,508
Have you got any other cool projects that you'd like to share?

180
00:14:34,126 --> 00:14:35,684
Yeah, thanks for asking.

181
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:43,076
in Italy American RD here, we're proud of ourselves launching this research program called GLOW.

182
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:45,520
we try to glow our grid.

183
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:52,868
The program is all about creating the next generation of digital solutions for our power grid users.

184
00:14:53,600 --> 00:15:01,600
because 10 years ago, we started to see the complexity increase when you have inverter-based resources coming online.

185
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:06,480
For example, renewables, the energy storage, the battery.

186
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:15,588
Along with that, the grid operation becomes more complicated, and the planning process becomes even more complicated.

187
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,400
so we can't just blindly increase the size of our infrastructure.

188
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:25,028
We have to do that in a very organized, well-thought-through process.

189
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:30,400
And 10 years ago, we felt that AI could play an important role here.

190
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:48,800
So we started the program and targeted ourselves to innovate in the physical AI-based field, so that we can support great operators, great planners, and decision makers in this journey, so that they can accelerate their decision process.

191
00:15:49,070 --> 00:15:56,000
they can analyze much more complexity, and then make smarter decisions much, much faster than before.

192
00:15:56,174 --> 00:15:57,988
with the help of the physical AI.

193
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:08,548
but we do keep in mind that AI is a gear that people can leverage to winning the trust of the users.

194
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:15,360
They have to follow the same criteria as any other physical-based tools that people are using today.

195
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:22,000
So they have to be reliable, they have to be accuracy, they have to be repeatable and auditable.

196
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:29,732
all these requirements only appear on the paper, but they're mandated for energy industry.

197
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:36,720
because reliability is the top concern, and millions of people depend on it every day.

198
00:16:37,934 --> 00:16:40,080
because AI is still pretty new, all things considered.

199
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:46,880
when you go into a project like this, are you able to pinpoint exactly how long it'll take you to get to the end of it with AI?

200
00:16:47,150 --> 00:16:48,560
Or are you still learning as you go along?

201
00:16:48,846 --> 00:16:51,840
Or do you know exactly it'll take me three weeks, four weeks, you know, per phase?

202
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:53,280
You mean the implementation?

203
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:54,800
Yes.

204
00:16:55,360 --> 00:17:01,808
I think the you know The constraints here is not when to finish the project.

205
00:17:02,158 --> 00:17:10,243
The real constraints here actually is can you finish the implementation of AI by winning the trust of the users?

206
00:17:10,608 --> 00:17:15,568
Because the smartest AI in the world won't be useful if the engineers don't trust it.

207
00:17:15,647 --> 00:17:15,808
Right.

208
00:17:16,493 --> 00:17:18,608
So here technology is not the end goal.

209
00:17:18,768 --> 00:17:28,260
The end goal is to help the users to make their decisions, solve their industry problems much sooner, but also in a trustable, reliable way.

210
00:17:29,168 --> 00:17:34,448
So implementing AI to help the users is a process.

211
00:17:34,766 --> 00:17:47,488
But the end goal is to empower them to make important and great decisions much faster, but without losing the engineering rigor that they were mandated to have.

212
00:17:47,886 --> 00:17:56,208
So you supply the software and you teach the engineers to use the prompts and the correct information that they need to get the best out of their operations.

213
00:17:56,528 --> 00:18:04,740
I think a lot of people mix the way they work with agentic AI tools versus the way that how physical AI works here.

214
00:18:05,168 --> 00:18:11,248
They do have a lot of commonalities and they do support people to make faster, better decisions.

215
00:18:11,488 --> 00:18:20,368
But the fundamental difference here is in physical AI, we're combining AI grounded with physics and engineering principles.

216
00:18:20,928 --> 00:18:28,208
So to the users, to the power engineers, this is no different from using another physics-based solution.

217
00:18:28,768 --> 00:18:35,968
So they need to use physics-based AI tools with the same confidence as they're using in the legacy tools.

218
00:18:37,008 --> 00:18:37,328
Okay.

219
00:18:37,678 --> 00:18:45,860
So Bo, can you tell me a little bit more about physical AI, what it means exactly, and how Hitachi helps its customers with the implementation?

220
00:18:46,702 --> 00:18:52,996
Yeah, physical AI is a unique sector of the AI industry.

221
00:18:53,838 --> 00:19:04,740
So in Hitachi, we are combining AI deeply embedded with physics and engineering principles, not just pattern recognition.

222
00:19:05,248 --> 00:19:15,204
This is very important for the energy industry because traditionally engineers make decisions based on physics and engineering principles.

223
00:19:15,662 --> 00:19:18,528
So they were able to achieve high accuracy.

224
00:19:18,688 --> 00:19:29,568
And in the past decades, because all the challenges we just talked about, the legacy tools is not able to fulfill the requirement for today's analysis.

225
00:19:29,808 --> 00:19:42,288
So physical AI can play a game-changing role here because we can leverage the benefits of AI without losing the engineering rigor that the industry will demand for.

226
00:19:42,608 --> 00:19:49,124
So, Bo, when it comes to our offering in physical AI, how does Hitachi differentiate itself from competitors?

227
00:19:49,678 --> 00:19:59,332
One thing that I feel really differentiates Hitachi from the other players here is that we have this philosophy that technology is not the end goal.

228
00:19:59,630 --> 00:20:16,772
The end goal here is to enable and empower our users, engineers in the power industry to work with a physical AI tools with more confidence, make smarter, better decisions faster.

229
00:20:17,168 --> 00:20:26,180
So, with that, we actually follow a very unique quality control process in our AI implementation.

230
00:20:26,528 --> 00:20:32,356
So the AI tools have to be reliable, repeatable, and auditable.

231
00:20:32,622 --> 00:20:41,988
So these are not features to have, these are requirements, these are mandate, and they're extremely important for our energy industry users.

232
00:20:43,534 --> 00:20:50,336
So if we can go back to Southwest Power Pool, can you tell me what results you achieved by the implementation of this physical AI?

233
00:20:50,702 --> 00:20:56,256
Hitachi's role is to help to modernize the analysis.

234
00:20:56,496 --> 00:21:06,372
So we, collaborating with SPP and under the Wan Hitachi team, we were able to dramatically accelerate the simulation, the study time.

235
00:21:06,576 --> 00:21:14,980
For example, for contingency analysis, we were able to reduce the simulation time from weeks to a few minutes.

236
00:21:15,566 --> 00:21:17,936
So this is a 100 times speed up.

237
00:21:18,256 --> 00:21:28,176
And also combined with other type of AI tools, we're able to reduce the generation review process from 18 months to within a year.

238
00:21:28,576 --> 00:21:43,236
So this helps bring more generation, new generation online much sooner, clear out the queue, help the new generation to play in the power grid so that they can meet the demand of the AI data center load.

239
00:21:44,366 --> 00:21:48,900
So data centers, do you see a big role for them in the future grid or the grid of the future?

240
00:21:49,486 --> 00:21:50,736
Are they the biggest player, do you think?

241
00:21:51,056 --> 00:21:58,468
I think data center loads, especially the AI data centers, we will look at them very differently several decades from now.

242
00:21:59,456 --> 00:22:05,376
So today we're still considering data center as another large consumer.

243
00:22:05,614 --> 00:22:09,060
They draw enormous amount of energy from the power grid.

244
00:22:10,016 --> 00:22:29,376
But I think they will integrate with the power grid more tightly in the future so that we can leverage advanced energy management, fast computing, intelligent control, so that we can incorporate data center to support the grid when the grid is in need.

245
00:22:29,710 --> 00:22:39,844
Their role will change from a passive load consumer, energy consumer to a proactive participant in the energy ecosystem.

246
00:22:41,006 --> 00:22:45,988
And looking ahead well into the future, what do you think the grid looks like?

247
00:22:46,670 --> 00:22:54,336
Yeah, to me, the future grid will definitely be more digital, more intelligent, and more adaptive.

248
00:22:54,830 --> 00:22:59,588
I think we'll see tighter integration between IT and OT.

249
00:22:59,776 --> 00:23:07,492
We will be able to provide the grid operators more real-time visibility and support them to make smarter decisions.

250
00:23:07,936 --> 00:23:18,576
And then, you know, with all the good features from digital tools, AI, and the smart controls, we were able to enable the grid to be more adaptive as well.

251
00:23:18,798 --> 00:23:33,796
So the grid will be more smart to accommodate new generations, new load, and uh extreme weathers, whatever comes next, despite all these challenges, to maintain the reliability and affordability for the consumers.

252
00:23:34,096 --> 00:23:36,612
So this is the most exciting part to me.

253
00:23:36,846 --> 00:23:45,572
I feel that we're not only modernize our infrastructure, we're actually building our infrastructure ready for the next generation.

254
00:23:46,446 --> 00:23:47,376
Thank you so much.

255
00:23:47,598 --> 00:23:48,976
